Fix a lost wake-up in the non-futex QMutex (Windows and macOS, on arm64)

QBasicMutex::unlockInternal() with no waiter does
  (2) waiters.fetchAndAddRelease(-BigNumber)
  (3) d_ptr.testAndSetRelease(d, 0)
  (4) derefWaiters(0): waiters.testAndSetRelaxed(-BigNumber, 0)
and a concurrent lockInternal() does
  (1) old = waiters.loadAcquire(), (5) waiters.testAndSetRelaxed(old, old + 1),
  (6) if (d != d_ptr.loadAcquire()) retry; else d->wait().
If (1) or (5) reads the 0 written by (4), (6) must see the d_ptr reset of
(3). With (4) and (5) relaxed nothing guarantees it, and on arm64 (6) can
still read d: the locking thread then sleeps on a QMutexPrivate that no
unlocker will ever wake (d_ptr == 0, waiters == 1).
 - (1) reads (4): QTBUG-88247 (d08e3b6de161) made (1) an acquire, but (4)
   is not a release, so there is no synchronization.
 - (5) reads (4) after (1) read the 0 from before (2) (ABA): (5) is not an
   acquire.
Make (4) a release and (5) an acquire, so that (3) happens before (6)
in both cases.

--- a/src/corelib/thread/qmutex.cpp
+++ b/src/corelib/thread/qmutex.cpp
@@ -631,7 +631,9 @@
                     break;
                 }
             }
-        } while (!d->waiters.testAndSetRelaxed(old_waiters, old_waiters + 1));
+            // Acquire: if this reads the 0 that derefWaiters() wrote in unlockInternal(), the
+            // d_ptr check below must see that unlockInternal() reset d_ptr just before.
+        } while (!d->waiters.testAndSetAcquire(old_waiters, old_waiters + 1));
 
         if (d != d_ptr.loadAcquire()) {
             // The mutex was unlocked before we incremented waiters.
@@ -760,7 +762,11 @@
             new_waiters += QMutexPrivate::BigNumber;
         }
         new_waiters -= value;
-    } while (!waiters.testAndSetRelaxed(old_waiters, new_waiters));
+        // Release, so that a thread in lockInternal() that reads this new value with
+        // waiters.loadAcquire() also sees the d_ptr reset that unlockInternal() did just
+        // before. With a relaxed store, on weakly-ordered CPUs (arm64), it could see
+        // waiters == 0 but the old d_ptr, and wait forever on an unlocked mutex.
+    } while (!waiters.testAndSetRelease(old_waiters, new_waiters));
 }
 #endif
 
